From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 18:03:09 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] HID: surface: Ignore Fn usages and map Call Mute Toggle to
 KEY_MICMUTE

The Surface Pro 9 Type Cover keyboard (045E:09AE) has a System Control
input report with three one-bit fields: System Function Shift (0x97),
System Function Shift Lock (0x98) and System Function Shift Lock
Indicator (0x99). Fn and Fn lock are handled by the cover firmware,
which already switches the top row between F1-F12 and the media keys,
and the host cannot set the lock state. hid-input does not know these
usages and maps them to BTN_0, BTN_1 and BTN_2. Only BTN_0 is filtered
by this driver, so Fn still emits BTN_1 and BTN_2, and if the firmware
keeps the lock bits set while Fn lock is on, those buttons stay pressed.

Do not map the three usages at all.

The same keyboard has a one-bit Call Mute Toggle (Generic Desktop
0xe1) in a Call State Management Control collection. hid-input has no
mapping for it either and turns it into the next free BTN_* code. Map
it to KEY_MICMUTE, the code hid-input uses for System Microphone Mute
(0xa9).

Signed-off-by: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/hid/hid-surface.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-surface.c b/drivers/hid/hid-surface.c
index a171ea656..5e4b6359a 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-surface.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-surface.c
@@ -12,6 +12,11 @@
 
 #include "hid-ids.h"
 
+#define SURFACE_USAGE_FN_SHIFT		0x00010097
+#define SURFACE_USAGE_FN_LOCK		0x00010098
+#define SURFACE_USAGE_FN_LOCK_INDICATOR	0x00010099
+#define SURFACE_USAGE_CALL_MUTE_TOGGLE	0x000100e1
+
 /*
  * The Surface Aggregator Module firmware incorrectly reports the FN key
  * as BTN_0 (button 256). This button can get stuck in pressed state,
@@ -23,6 +28,16 @@ static int surface_input_mapping(struct hid_device *hdev, struct hid_input *hi,
 				  struct hid_field *field, struct hid_usage *usage,
 				  unsigned long **bit, int *max)
 {
+	switch (usage->hid) {
+	case SURFACE_USAGE_FN_SHIFT:
+	case SURFACE_USAGE_FN_LOCK:
+	case SURFACE_USAGE_FN_LOCK_INDICATOR:
+		return -1;
+	case SURFACE_USAGE_CALL_MUTE_TOGGLE:
+		hid_map_usage_clear(hi, usage, bit, max, EV_KEY, KEY_MICMUTE);
+		return 1;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Filter BTN_0 during input mapping in case it appears in the
 	 * HID descriptor (defense in depth).
